# Xcode
build/
DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate
*.xcscmblueprint
*.moved-aside
*.hmap
*.ipa
*.dSYM.zip
*.dSYM

# Per-user Xcode state. Shared schemes live in xcshareddata and ARE committed:
# a test target with no shared scheme means CI silently runs zero tests.
xcuserdata/

# Ruby toolchain. Gemfile.lock IS committed (it pins CocoaPods and every gem,
# with checksums); the installed gems themselves are not.
#
# Both cases: the directory on disk is `Vendor/`, and this only matched it because
# `core.ignorecase` happens to be true on a case-insensitive volume. On a case-sensitive
# checkout the lowercase pattern alone silently stops ignoring anything, and ~2000 gem
# files appear as untracked.
Vendor/bundle/
vendor/bundle/
.bundle/

# CocoaPods
#
# Pods/ IS committed deliberately — see Vendor/libsignal/DECISIONS.md.
# It makes the libsignal Swift wrapper diffable on every dependency bump, which
# matters for a cryptographic dependency. Note it does NOT contain the Rust
# binary: libsignal_ffi.a is downloaded at build time and pinned by checksum.
#
# Nothing to ignore here; this block exists so the choice is not mistaken for an
# oversight by a future reader.

# The libsignal source snapshot that predated proper dependency management.
# Deleted in M1; this entry stops it reappearing.
libsignal-main.zip

# Python bytecode. Scripts/verify-localization.py is imported by maintenance one-offs,
# which leaves a __pycache__ behind next to the gate itself.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Per-developer AI agent tooling. Local wiring (which MCP server to launch), not
# project source, and it differs per machine and per tool. Ignored rather than
# left untracked because `git add -A` is a step in the pre-commit secret scan
# (AGENT_HANDOFF.md §7), so anything untracked gets staged by whoever runs it
# next. The contract an agent works to IS committed: docs/AGENT_HANDOFF.md.
.codex/

# Relay secrets. server/.env holds the Postgres and Redis passwords; server/.env.example
# is the tracked template and holds none.
#
# The pattern is anchored and broad on purpose: this repository is public, so a
# committed .env is a credential disclosure that survives in history after the file
# is deleted. `git rm --cached` does not undo a push.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The relay binary, if someone builds outside the container.
/server/relay
